# name: (summary, life)
D={
"Pangaea and the age of dinosaurs":("From about 335 to 175 million years ago most land formed one supercontinent, Pangaea, surrounded by the Panthalassa ocean. The Mesozoic Era (252–66 million years ago) — Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous — began after the Great Dying, the worst mass extinction, and saw dinosaurs dominate land, pterosaurs the air and marine reptiles the seas. Mammals appeared about 200 million years ago but stayed small and mostly nocturnal; birds evolved from feathered dinosaurs, and flowering plants spread in the Cretaceous. India drifted north as an island, and the Deccan Traps erupted near the end.","No humans existed; the ancestors of mammals were shrew-sized creatures living in the shadows of dinosaurs, feeding on insects and seeds at night."),
"Age of mammals (Cenozoic)":("After the Chicxulub impact 66 million years ago, mammals diversified into the niches left by dinosaurs: early whales evolved in the Tethys Sea near India and Pakistan (Pakicetus), horses, elephants and primates appeared, and grasslands spread as the climate cooled. India collided with Asia, raising the Himalaya and creating the monsoon. Apes evolved in Africa and Eurasia around 25–20 million years ago, and the human line split from chimpanzees about 7 million years ago.","Primates lived in forests, eating fruit and leaves; as forests fragmented in Africa, some apes began to walk upright between patches of trees."),
"Australopithecines":("Between about 4.2 and 2 million years ago, Australopithecus species such as anamensis, afarensis (Lucy) and africanus lived in eastern and southern Africa. They walked upright with an arched foot but still had long arms for climbing and ape-sized brains of 400–500 cm³. The Laetoli footprints and Lucy's skeleton (found 1974 at Hadar) are their most famous evidence; some may have used sharp stones to butcher meat. Robust Paranthropus with huge chewing teeth lived alongside early Homo.","Small groups foraged in woodland and grassland mosaics for fruit, roots, nuts, termites and occasional meat, sleeping in trees for safety from big cats and hyenas."),
"Lower Paleolithic":("The Lower Paleolithic, from the first stone tools 3.3 million years ago to about 300,000 years ago, is the longest chapter of human history. Oldowan choppers gave way to Acheulean hand-axes around 1.76 million years ago, made by Homo erectus, who spread out of Africa to Georgia, Java, China and India (Attirampakkam, about 1.5 million years ago). Control of fire appears by about 1 million years ago (Wonderwerk Cave), and wooden spears at Schöningen show big-game hunting by 300,000 years ago.","Small mobile bands of perhaps 20–50 people hunted, scavenged and gathered; fire for warmth and cooking gradually became central, and making complex tools suggests teaching and early communication."),
"Middle Paleolithic":("From about 300,000 to 40,000 years ago, Neanderthals in Europe and western Asia and early Homo sapiens in Africa made tools with the Levallois technique, striking prepared cores to get flakes of a planned shape. Homo sapiens appeared by 315,000 years ago (Jebel Irhoud); Denisovans lived in Asia. People buried their dead, used ochre and shell beads, made glue from birch tar, and spread Out of Africa around 70,000–50,000 years ago, interbreeding with Neanderthals and Denisovans.","Groups lived in caves and open camps, hunting deer, bison and horses with hafted spears, gathering plants and shellfish, and caring for injured or elderly members, as Neanderthal skeletons with healed injuries show."),
"Upper Paleolithic":("Between about 50,000 and 12,000 years ago, Homo sapiens spread across the globe, reaching Australia, Siberia and the Americas. Blade tools, bone needles, spear-throwers, bows and harpoons appeared, along with explosive creativity: cave art at Sulawesi, Chauvet and Lascaux, the Venus figurines, bone flutes and personal ornaments. Neanderthals disappeared around 40,000 years ago, and dogs were domesticated. In India, Bhimbetka's rock shelters contain some of the oldest art.","Hunter-gatherers lived in larger, networked bands, sewing fitted clothing, building mammoth-bone huts in Ukraine, trading shells and stone over hundreds of kilometres, and holding feasts and rituals."),
"Last Glacial Maximum":("Around 26,000–19,000 years ago ice sheets reached their maximum. Sea level was about 120 m lower, opening land bridges like Beringia; much of northern Europe was polar desert and people retreated to southern refugia. Deserts expanded, and in India the Thar was larger and drier. Mammoths, woolly rhinos and reindeer roamed the steppe-tundra.","People survived in sheltered valleys, hunting reindeer and mammoth, storing meat in permafrost and using fur clothing, lamps and needles."),
"Mesolithic":("The Mesolithic (Middle Stone Age), from about 12,000 to the arrival of farming (varying by region), followed the Ice Age. Forests spread over Europe, and people adapted with microliths — tiny flint blades set into arrows and harpoons — fishing and canoes. In India, sites like Bagor, Langhnaj and Sarai Nahar Rai show microlithic hunters and early burials, and Bhimbetka's paintings depict hunting and dancing.","Communities gathered seasonally by rivers, lakes and coasts, eating fish, shellfish, deer, nuts and wild grains; some became semi-settled, like the Natufians of the Levant."),
"Neolithic Revolution":("Beginning about 10,000 BCE in the Fertile Crescent and independently in China, New Guinea, Mesoamerica, the Andes, Africa and India, people domesticated plants and animals. Wheat, barley, lentils, sheep and goats came first in West Asia; rice and millet in China; maize in Mexico. In South Asia, Mehrgarh (from about 7000 BCE) grew barley and wheat and kept cattle. Farming fed more people, leading to villages, pottery, property and later inequality, though early farmers were often shorter and less healthy than foragers.","Families lived in mud-brick or wattle houses, grew grain, kept animals, ground flour on stones, made pottery and polished stone axes, and buried ancestors under house floors."),
"Chalcolithic (Copper Age)":("From about 5000 to 3000 BCE (later in some regions), people used copper alongside stone. Copper smelting appeared in Serbia, Iran and Anatolia; Ötzi the Iceman (about 3300 BCE) carried a copper axe. In India, Chalcolithic cultures such as Ahar-Banas, Malwa and Jorwe flourished, and early Harappan villages grew. The wheel, plough and wool sheep spread, and the first towns appeared in Mesopotamia.","Villagers farmed with ox-drawn ploughs, made painted pottery, traded copper and shell, and began to live in larger towns with craft specialists and chiefs."),
"Bronze Age":("Bronze — copper alloyed with tin — became widespread from about 3300 BCE in the Near East, Indus Valley and Aegean, and later in China and Europe. Cities, writing, kings, temples, armies and long-distance trade developed: Sumer, Egypt's pyramids, the Indus cities, Shang China and Minoan Crete. Tin had to be traded over long distances, linking civilisations. Horses and chariots spread around 2000 BCE.","Society became stratified: kings, priests and scribes at the top; artisans, merchants and farmers below; and slaves at the bottom. Farmers paid taxes in grain, and many worked on state building projects."),
"4.2-kiloyear event":("Around 2200 BCE a mega-drought lasting about two centuries affected regions from Egypt to India and China, coinciding with the end of the Akkadian Empire and Egypt's Old Kingdom and stress on the Indus cities. The International Commission on Stratigraphy uses it to mark the start of the Meghalayan Age, named after Mawmluh cave in Meghalaya, whose stalagmites record it.","Farmers abandoned failing fields and migrated; cities shrank, and central governments lost legitimacy as they failed to feed people."),
"Late Bronze Age collapse":("Around 1200–1150 BCE, the interconnected world of the eastern Mediterranean fell apart: the Hittite Empire collapsed, Mycenaean palaces burned, Ugarit was destroyed, and Egypt was weakened by the Sea Peoples. Drought, earthquakes, famine, migrations, rebellions and the breakdown of trade networks probably combined in a 'systems collapse'. Writing disappeared in Greece for centuries.","Survivors lived in smaller villages, and trade dried up; out of the chaos came iron-working, the Phoenician alphabet, and new peoples such as the Israelites and Philistines."),
"Iron Age":("Iron working, harder to master than bronze but using common ore, spread after about 1200 BCE across the Near East and Europe, and independently or earlier in India (iron at sites in the Ganga valley and south India by 1800–1500 BCE) and in Africa (Nok, Meroë). Cheap iron tools and weapons let farmers clear forests and armies equip more soldiers. In India the Iron Age saw the later Vedic period and the rise of the mahajanapadas; in Europe, Celts and the Greek city-states.","Iron axes and ploughshares expanded farming into forests like the Ganga plain; iron weapons equipped larger infantry armies, and new kingdoms and cities formed."),
"Vedic period":("The Vedic period (about 1500–500 BCE) is named after the Vedas, composed in Sanskrit by Indo-Aryan-speaking people in the northwest and later the Ganga plain. Early clans herded cattle and fought over pastures; later kingdoms (janapadas) formed, the varna system developed, and the Upanishads explored philosophy. Iron, rice farming and cities emerged by its end.","Families lived in villages of mud and thatch, kept cattle as wealth, drank milk and ghee, performed fire sacrifices, and held assemblies called sabha and samiti."),
"Axial Age":("Between about 800 and 200 BCE, new philosophies and religions emerged almost simultaneously: the Upanishads, Buddha and Mahavira in India; Confucius and Laozi in China; Zoroaster in Persia; Hebrew prophets; and Greek philosophers from Thales to Aristotle. Karl Jaspers named it the Axial Age. These traditions questioned ritual and emphasised ethics, personal transformation and reason.","Growing cities, trade, coinage and literacy gave rise to wandering teachers, debates and schools; ordinary people still farmed but heard new ideas about how to live."),
"Classical antiquity":("Classical antiquity in the Mediterranean ran from about the 8th century BCE to the 5th century CE — Greece's city-states and philosophers, Alexander's Hellenistic world, and the Roman Republic and Empire. It produced democracy, theatre, history, geometry, Roman law, roads and concrete, and the spread of Christianity. It ended with the fall of the Western Roman Empire.","City life included public baths, theatres, markets and temples; most people were farmers, and slavery was widespread."),
"Mauryan and post-Mauryan India":("From 322 BCE the Mauryas united most of India; after 185 BCE the Shungas, Satavahanas, Indo-Greeks, Shakas and Kushans ruled different regions. Trade with Rome boomed, Gandhara and Mathura art flourished, Buddhism spread, and the Sangam poets wrote in Tamil.","Cities like Pataliputra, Taxila and Ujjain hosted guilds of merchants and artisans; farmers grew rice, wheat and cotton; and monasteries became centres of learning."),
"Han and Roman era":("From about 200 BCE to 200 CE, the Han dynasty in China and the Roman Empire were the world's two great empires, each with about 60 million people. The Silk Road linked them through Parthia and the Kushans. Both built roads, standardised coins and administered vast territories, and both fell into crisis in the 3rd century.","Peasants paid taxes in grain and labour; soldiers guarded frontiers; and silk, spices and glass moved across Eurasia."),
"Classical Golden Age of India (Gupta)":("From about 320 to 550 CE the Guptas ruled north India in an age of Sanskrit literature (Kalidasa), mathematics (Aryabhata), astronomy, temple architecture and Ajanta painting. Nalanda was founded. Huna invasions ended it.","Faxian described a peaceful kingdom with free hospitals; guilds were strong, and land grants to Brahmins and temples grew."),
"Late Antique Little Ice Age":("Volcanic eruptions in 536, 540 and 547 CE cooled the Northern Hemisphere for over a century. Harvest failures, the Plague of Justinian and migrations coincided with the decline of the Byzantine and Sasanian empires and the rise of the Arabs.","Farmers faced cold summers and famine; plague emptied cities."),
"Early Middle Ages":("From about 500 to 1000 CE, Europe's post-Roman kingdoms, the Byzantine Empire, the rise of Islam, Tang China, and India's regional kingdoms such as the Chalukyas, Pallavas, Rashtrakutas and Palas.","Most people farmed under lords, with monasteries preserving learning."),
"Islamic Golden Age":("From the 8th to the 13th century, Baghdad, Cairo and Córdoba were centres of science: algebra, optics (Ibn al-Haytham), medicine (Ibn Sina), and translations of Greek and Indian works.","Cities had libraries, hospitals and markets; paper made books cheap."),
"Medieval Warm Period":("From about 950 to 1250 a warmer climate in the North Atlantic helped Vikings settle Greenland and European population grow.","Better harvests and new lands supported growing towns."),
"Chola and medieval India":("From the 9th to 13th centuries, the Cholas built a naval empire, temples rose across India, and the Delhi Sultanate began.","Temple towns organised irrigation, schools and festivals."),
"Mongol era":("In the 13th–14th centuries the Mongol Empire linked Eurasia, spreading trade, technology and plague.","Nomads and city-dwellers alike faced conquest, then new trade."),
"Black Death era":("From 1346 to 1353 plague killed a third to half of Europe and many in Asia and Africa.","Labour shortages raised wages and weakened feudalism."),
"Little Ice Age":("From about 1300 to 1850 cooler temperatures brought famines and harsh winters.","Crop failures and frozen rivers shaped daily life."),
"Delhi Sultanate and Vijayanagara":("From 1206 to 1526 the Delhi Sultanate ruled north India, while Vijayanagara rose in the south.","Bhakti and Sufi saints preached devotion across castes."),
"Renaissance":("From the 14th to 17th century Europe revived classical learning, art and science.","Printing spread knowledge; patrons funded art."),
"Age of Exploration":("From the 15th to 17th century Europeans sailed to the Americas, Africa and Asia.","Ships carried goods, enslaved people and diseases."),
"Mughal era":("From 1526 to 1857 the Mughals ruled much of India.","Cities like Agra and Delhi flourished; peasants paid heavy revenue."),
"Colonial era":("From the 16th to 20th centuries European empires ruled much of the world.","Colonised peoples faced exploitation and resistance."),
"Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment":("From the 16th to 18th centuries science and reason transformed thought.","Coffee houses and salons spread ideas."),
"Industrial Revolution":("From about 1760, machines, steam and factories transformed production.","Workers moved to cities and worked long hours."),
"British Raj":("From 1858 to 1947 the British Crown ruled India.","Famines, railways and the freedom movement shaped life."),
"World wars era":("From 1914 to 1945 two world wars killed tens of millions.","Rationing, conscription and bombing affected civilians."),
"Cold War":("From 1947 to 1991 the US and USSR competed globally.","Fear of nuclear war shaped politics."),
"Information Age":("Since the 1970s computers and the internet transformed life.","Smartphones connect billions."),
"Anthropocene":("A proposed epoch in which humans dominate Earth's systems.","Climate change and biodiversity loss affect everyone."),
}
